        {
          ":db/id": 17592186065198,
          ":step/content": {
            ":db/id": 17592186065339,
            ":tutorial/content": "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$",
            ":tutorial/id": "40488864-6672-59b7-9ac6-79903856b484",
            ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 1481,
            ":tutorial/title": "The Power Rule With Fractional Exponents"
          },
          ":step/id": "19a0572e-4edb-579e-b72d-fdc1fefe72ca",
          ":step/math-academy-id": 3536,
